# notion-sync workspace

This folder is data synced from Notion by [notion-sync](https://github.com/Drexel-UHC/notion-sync). This file tells LLM/agent tools how to read it and how to write back safely. It is **generated and tool-owned** — do not hand-edit; `import` / `refresh` overwrite it on the next sync.

**Full docs for the binary that wrote this folder:** {{DOCS_URL}} — machine-readable index at `{{DOCS_URL}}llms.txt` (every page is also served as `.md`). This file keeps only what you need offline; everything else is a link below. If the site and this file disagree, this file describes the binary that actually wrote the files.

## Layout

```
<workspace-root>/
├── AGENTS.md                      <-- this file
├── <Database Title>/
│   ├── _database.json             (databaseId, dataSourceId, title, url, lastSyncedAt, entryCount, syncVersion)
│   └── <notion-id>.md             (one per database entry)
├── <Multi-Source Database>/
│   ├── _database.json             (top-level, no dataSourceId)
│   └── <Source Title>/
│       ├── _database.json         (with dataSourceId)
│       └── <notion-id>.md
└── pages/
    └── <Page Title>_<short-id>/   (standalone page imported by URL)
        ├── _page.json
        └── <Page Title>.md
```

- Database entries are named `{notion-id}.md` — stable across renames; the page title lives in the frontmatter's title property.
- Details: [Workspace layout]({{DOCS_URL}}reference/workspace-layout) · [Metadata files]({{DOCS_URL}}reference/metadata-files)

## Frontmatter

Every `.md` starts with YAML frontmatter: notion-sync metadata first, then the Notion page's properties verbatim.

```yaml
---
notion-id: "<page-uuid>"
notion-url: "https://app.notion.com/p/<page-id-32-hex>"
notion-last-edited: "<RFC 3339 — Notion's last_edited_time>"
notion-database-id: "<database-uuid>"   # database entries only
# notion-deleted: true                  # entry was removed in Notion (soft delete)
# notion-last-pushed: "<RFC 3339>"      # present after a push
# notion-create: true                   # a new row to create on next push (no notion-id)
Title Property: "Page Name"
Status: "In Progress"
---
```

- **Soft deletes:** pages removed from Notion stay on disk with `notion-deleted: true`. Treat them as historical.
- **File URLs:** `files` properties and body embeds carry Notion S3 URLs with the signed query string stripped. The path is a **stable identifier** but a GET **will not return bytes** — re-query the Notion API for a fresh signed URL. External URLs pass through verbatim.
- **Refresh:** `refresh` is incremental by timestamp; `refresh --force` resyncs all; `refresh --ids a,b` resyncs specific pages. `clean` fixes up files with no API call.
- Full property → frontmatter table (and which types are pushable): [Frontmatter]({{DOCS_URL}}reference/frontmatter) · [File URLs]({{DOCS_URL}}reference/file-urls) · [Commands]({{DOCS_URL}}reference/commands)

## Push — default to NOT pushing

`notion-sync push <folder>` writes frontmatter **property** changes back to Notion. Page body content is never touched. It is the **only** command that mutates the user's live Notion workspace, and its writes are not trivially reversible. Treat it as privileged; your job is to **satisfy** its guardrails, never to circumvent them.

**Every condition below must hold before you push. If any fails, do not push — stop and report the blocker and its fix to the human.**

1. **Explicit human authorization** — push only when a human asked you to, for this specific change. Never on your own initiative.
2. **Workspace in sync** — run `refresh` first and resolve any conflict (drift) at the source. A conflict means Notion changed under you; reconcile it, don't override it.
3. **Reviewed dry run** — run `push --dry-run`, show the human what would change, and get their OK before the real push.
4. **Validation gate clean** — no halt-class files (table below). A halt means *stop and report*, not *force through*.
5. **Human consent to the write** — the confirmation gate is that checkpoint. Interactive runs prompt `y/N` (default N). Non-interactive runs (piped / CI / agent) **require `--yes`** or cancel with `status: "cancelled"`. Pass `--yes` **only after a human reviewed the dry run and approved this push**. Treat "cancelled" as "ask the human," not "retry with `--yes`."

**Never silence a guardrail to make a push succeed.** `--force` (skips the whole validation gate, local blind-overwrites Notion), `--allow-new-options` (creates new select/multi-select options in the user's schema), and a reflexive `--yes` all suppress protections that exist for the user's data — use them only on explicit, per-run human instruction, never to clear a halt you hit on your own. When in doubt, **do not push — hand back to the human.**

### Validation gate — all-or-nothing; a halt means stop

If **any** file in the folder is a halt class, the **entire run aborts before a single write** (`status: "halted"`, non-zero exit). Report the reason and fix; do not reach for `--force`.

| Halt class | Meaning | Fix |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `conflict` | Notion's row changed since your last sync | `refresh` (take Notion's values) or, on explicit human instruction only, `push --force` |
| `stray` | `.md` with no `notion-id` and no `notion-create: true` marker | remove it, add a valid `notion-id`, or mark it for creation |
| `create-pending` | `notion-create: pending` — a previous push may already have created this row | human step: `refresh`; if the row arrived as `<id>.md`, delete the pending file; else set the marker back to `true`. **Never flip `pending` back to `true` without checking first.** |
| `invalid-option` | select / status / multi_select value not in the schema | use an existing option (or `--allow-new-options`, human-authorized, select/multi only) |
| `malformed` | frontmatter unparsable, or both `notion-create: true` and a `notion-id` | fix the frontmatter |
| `unreadable` / `unreachable` | file unreadable, or Notion unreachable | fix permissions / retry later |

### Creating new rows

A `.md` with **no `notion-id`** and `notion-create: true` plus the database's title property is created as a new row on push — **properties only**, never the body. Just before the create call the marker becomes `notion-create: pending`; on success the file is linked in place (marker removed, `notion-id` etc. added) and listed in the summary's `"created"` array. If it stays `pending`, see the `create-pending` row above — recovery is a human step.

### Run summary — parse the first JSON object on stdout

Every terminal path prints one JSON object first on stdout (`status`: `clean` | `partial` | `halted` | `cancelled`; arrays `pushed`, `created`, `skippedNoOp`, `skippedNonRow`, `failed`, `halted`, always present). Preview, progress and the prompt go to stderr. Exit code is `0` for `clean` / `cancelled`, non-zero for `partial` / `halted` — a dry run can still halt. **On any `halted`, `partial`, or `cancelled` outcome, report each entry's `reason` and `fix` to the human and stop** — do not auto-retry with `--force` / `--allow-new-options`. Whether to override a guardrail is the human's decision, not yours.

### Rich-text fidelity on push

`title` / `rich_text` cells are flat Markdown in your `.md`. Bold, italic, code, strikethrough, highlight, underline and links round-trip. Two attributes are lost at import and can **never be restored on push**: **Foreground (text) color** and `@user` **mention identity** (rendered as `@name`, ID not kept). **Inline equation** survives as literal `$expr$` text but stops rendering as math. Don't expect a pushed cell to restore them.

Mechanics, flags, full summary schema and examples: [Push guide]({{DOCS_URL}}guides/push) · [For agents]({{DOCS_URL}}agents)
